#!/usr/bin/env perl
use 5.020;
use strict;
use warnings;
use Ham::RTTY::_Internal::CLI;

local $SIG{PIPE} = 'IGNORE' if exists $SIG{PIPE};
exit Ham::RTTY::_Internal::CLI::run(\@ARGV, \*STDIN, \*STDOUT, \*STDERR);

=encoding UTF-8

=head1 NAME

ham-rtty - Morse text, event, WAV and MIDI command-line tools

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    ham-rtty encode "CQ DE TESTCALL"
    ham-rtty decode "... --- ..."
    ham-rtty events --wpm 20 "SOS"
    ham-rtty wav --output cq.wav --wpm 20 "CQ TEST"
    ham-rtty midi --output cq.mid "CQ TEST"
    ham-rtty receive --json cq.wav
    ham-rtty help receive

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Command-line interface to L<Ham::RTTY> for Morse conversion, WAV/MIDI output
and recorded CW reception. Requires Perl 5.20 or later and core modules;
PDL is optional. Audio reception is experimental: check its warnings.
Sound devices and live MIDI ports are outside its scope.

=head1 COMMANDS

=head2 encode

Convert text to Morse, with spaces between letters and C</> between words.
Accepts C<--unknown error|skip>; rejection is the default.

=head2 decode

Convert Morse groups to text. Use C<--prefer-prosigns> to choose named
operational prosigns for ambiguous groups. Separate letters with spaces
and words with C</>. Use C<-- > before Morse beginning with a dash.

=head2 events

Export canonical timed events as JSON. C<--sample-rate HZ> instead exports
sampled events. Accepts C<--wpm>, C<--effective-wpm> and C<--unknown>.

=head2 wav

Generate mono PCM WAV. C<--output FILE> or C<--output -> is required.
Accepts C<--wpm>, C<--effective-wpm>, C<--unknown>, C<--frequency>,
C<--sample-rate>, C<--amplitude>, C<--attack-ms>, C<--release-ms>,
C<--backend perl|pdl|auto>, C<--bits 8|16|24>, C<--block-size>,
C<--max-bytes> and C<--max-samples>. Values and defaults match L<Ham::RTTY>.

=head2 midi

Generate an SMF type 0 MIDI file. Explicit C<--output> is required.
Accepts C<--wpm>, C<--effective-wpm>, C<--unknown>, C<--note>,
C<--velocity>, C<--channel>, C<--program>, C<--bpm>, C<--ppqn> and
C<--max-bytes>. WPM controls Morse speed; BPM controls MIDI tick mapping.
Program numbers are zero-based, and channels are 1..16.

=head2 receive

Decode a PCM WAV recording. Supply one filename, C<--input FILE>, or
standard input when neither is given. C<-> also selects standard input.
Stdin is copied to a temporary seekable file and bounded by C<--max-bytes>
(64000000 by default); audio is then processed in bounded blocks.

Accepts C<--frequency>, C<--wpm>, C<--effective-wpm>, C<--backend>,
C<--stereo mix|left|right>, C<--max-frames>, C<--min-frequency>,
C<--max-frequency>, C<--frequency-step>, C<--min-contrast>,
C<--min-purity>, C<--filter-bandwidth>, C<--min-snr-db>, C<--mark-snr-db>, C<--all-stations>, C<--max-bytes> and C<--block-samples>.
C<--json> outputs the complete result, including events and warnings.
C<--all-stations> returns up to eight distinct filtered channel Results,
ordered by accumulated energy. With C<--json>, output is an array (possibly
empty); otherwise each line starts with the candidate frequency in Hz.
Without it, one primary Result is returned. WPM hints apply to all stations;
omit them for independent timing estimates. Station separation and filter
options follow L<Ham::RTTY::Detector>. Co-channel stations cannot be separated.
Otherwise text goes to stdout and warnings to stderr. C<--strict> returns
status 1 for any warning, even when text was recovered. Completion and
nonempty text do not certify reception accuracy.

=head1 INPUT AND OUTPUT

For text commands, supply positional words or C<--input FILE> (C<-i FILE>).
Combining both is an error. Omit both to read stdin. Positional words are
joined with one space. Text input is strict UTF-8, with an optional initial
BOM; text output is UTF-8 without a BOM, with LF line endings.
The default text limit is 8192 bytes. C<--max-input-bytes> accepts 1..1048576.
Library generation and reception limits apply independently.

Text/JSON output defaults to stdout. C<--output FILE> (C<-o FILE>) writes
a file. C<--output -> explicitly selects stdout, including binary output.
Existing paths are protected. C<--overwrite> explicitly replaces a named
file via a temporary sibling; failure preserves the previous contents.
Stdout cannot be rolled back after an I/O failure. Diagnostics use stderr
and never contaminate binary/JSON stdout. Broken pipes return status 3.

Options are case-sensitive and must be spelled in full. Repeated options
are rejected. C<-- > ends option parsing. Quote prosigns and shell special
characters; double quotes work in common Unix shells and Windows cmd.exe.
Non-ASCII positional arguments depend on the shell's UTF-8 support. UTF-8
files supplied with C<--input> provide portable non-ASCII input, including
on Windows. Windows installs also provide a C<ham-rtty.bat> launcher.

C<--help> and C<--version> work globally and after a command.
C<ham-rtty help COMMAND> shows an example and groups the available options
by purpose, with units and defaults. Usage errors point to the relevant help
page; help output fits a 78-column terminal without tab-dependent alignment.

=head1 EXIT STATUS

=over 4

=item C<0>

Successful conversion or reception with nonempty text.

=item C<1>

Reception produced no text, or C<--strict> found a warning.

=item C<2>

Invalid command, options or argument values.

=item C<3>

Invalid input data, a resource limit or an input/output failure.

=item C<4>

Unexpected internal failure. Report it with the command and a minimal input.

=back

Errors include their stable C<RTTY_E_*> code on stderr. Receive warnings use
C<RTTY_W_*> codes. See L<Ham::RTTY/ERRORS> for the library codes.

